// @run
// @log 1
// @log 7
// @hint Intentionally-unused variable `_v`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `apply` is inferred as `i32`
// CONTRACT (D939): a callee whose return row a `??` merges is reached even when the callee is
// ALSO passed as a function VALUE to a callback parameter — provided every caller of the
// function declaring that parameter passes the SAME function.
//
// `anonLeafParamArgsOk`'s function-value guard stands down whenever the program contains a call
// it cannot attribute, and a `call_ref` through a PARAMETER used to be unattributable by
// definition: `anonLeafFnValueTarget`'s header read "a `Param` named `nm` refutes outright: its
// value is whatever the caller passed". True of a parameter in general; not of one whose every
// caller passes one function name. That is D891's rung one frame further out — a parameter's
// sources are the arguments at every call site of the function that declares it.
//
// WHY THE DECLINE WAS NOT NEUTRAL, which is what made this a clause-1 cell rather than a
// refusal: with the read declined the `??` family kept ONE member, its atom set was a single
// atom, no union box was minted, and the default `{r: "s"}` then resolved by field NAME onto
// the narrow `{r: i32}` row — a string ref stored into an i32 field, check-clean invalid wasm.
//
// THE RE-ENTRY GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS MEASURED, not assumed: the resolver asks
// `anonLeafCalleeFnName` of every call site, which asks `anonLeafFnValueTarget`, which asks the
// resolver again — a cycle with no call frame between hops for the depth cap to count. Without
// the guard this exact program exhausted the COMPILER's own stack. Same lesson as D934's
// visited set, one resolver over.
function src(p: {r: i32}): {r: i32} | null {
  p
}

function apply(cb: ({r: i32}) => {r: i32} | null): i32 {
  // The callback's RESULT is scenery — what the cell needs is that the call HAPPENS, because
  // that `call_ref` is what the function-value guard sees.
  //
  // `apply`'s `: i32` IS LOAD-BEARING despite the hint calling it redundant, and it is declared
  // rather than deleted for that reason: without it the module builds and TRAPS at run time
  // with `indirect call type mismatch`, on master and here alike (a separate, pre-existing
  // clause-1 hole — see D940). The hint is about inference, not about the lowering.
  const _v = cb({ r: 9 })
  1
}

function rd() {
  print(apply(src))
  const g1 = src({ r: 7 }) ?? { r: "s" }
  print(g1.r)
}

rd()
